        "math_academy_id": "q-97794",
        "problem": "Consider the points $A(p,2p,3p)$ and $B(p,-2p,6p).$ Given that the distance between $A$ and $B$ is $15,$ find the possible values of $p.$",
        "worked_solution": "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(p,2p,3p)}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,\u00a0}{y}_{1}\\text{,\u00a0}{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(p,-2p,6p)}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,\u00a0}{y}_{2}\\text{,\u00a0}{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(p-p{)}^{2}+(-2p-2p{)}^{2}+(6p-3p{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4p{)}^{2}+(3p{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{p}^{2}+9{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\,|p|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=15,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\,|p| & =15 \\\\ |p| & =3 \\\\ p & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
